---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
import { chapterMarker, chapterNumber } from '../lib/tabs';
/* R270 [S12]: the eyebrow used to type this page's chapter number by hand,
   in a format ChapterNav did not use, next to a nav computing the same number
   from the registry. The number now comes from TABS and the format from
   chapterMarker, so reordering the registry moves both. chapterNumber throws
   here rather than rendering nothing, because a chapter whose page is not in
   the registry is a navigation dead end this page cannot detect. */
import { GOV_GROUPS, LEADER_BASIS_NOTE } from '../lib/gov';

/* R162 [§S12]: "84 entities under 9 governing bodies" was typed twice in this
   page's prose and computed from nothing. Both figures are right - the data
   holds 84 members across 9 groups - and both were the sixth instance of a
   count that agrees with its data until someone edits the data. The summary
   tiles below already derive the entity total from the same source; the
   sentences did not.
   The framework corroborates the nine (it carries a section on why nine
   institutions); it asserts no total for the entities, so 84 is what the
   plan's own architecture adds up to and is stated as such. */
const bodyCount = GOV_GROUPS.length;
const entityCount = GOV_GROUPS.reduce((n, g) => n + g.members.length, 0);
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Governance">
  <main>

  <section class="card chapter-intro">
    <span class="chapter-intro-eyebrow">{chapterMarker(chapterNumber('gov')!)} · Authority</span>
    <h2>Who operates, decides, audits, and enforces</h2>
    <p class="desc">Care, money, law, facilities, information, and people all
      need institutional owners, kept deliberately separate so no single
      body both runs the system and grades it. This chapter lays out the
      full governance architecture: {entityCount} entities under {bodyCount}
      governing bodies, each with a role, a reason, what it replaces, and
      who leads it. The
      next chapter shows how that distributed authority is engineered to
      survive a hostile administration.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The governance architecture</h2>
    <p class="desc">Replacing an industry means replacing its functions,
      and every function needs an owner. The plan defines {entityCount}
      entities organized under {bodyCount} governing bodies. The design follows two
      principles throughout: separate operations from oversight (the
      department that pays claims never judges its own appeals), and make
      the system hard to sabotage but easy to repair (mandatory payment
      rails, fixed-term appointments, vacancy-proof duties, an independent
      scorekeeper with a public formula registry). Each diagram below is
      one governing body; click any box for the full entry, including the
      position in charge and what it answers for.</p>
    <div class="tiles" id="gov-summary"></div>
    <p class="note" id="leader-basis-note">{LEADER_BASIS_NOTE}</p>
    <p class="note">Those proposals follow standard federal practice:
      Senate-confirmed heads for benefit-paying executive bodies, with
      nonexecutive scorekeeping and adjudication assigned only to
      constitutionally appropriate legislative offices or courts. After the
      Supreme Court's June 29, 2026 decision in <i>Trump v. Slaughter</i>,
      this dashboard does not assume that an executive regulator can be
      insulated from presidential removal. "Replaces" identifies the function
      absorbed, not a claim that the named organization vanishes on day one;
      the Transition body manages every handoff.</p>
  </section>

  <div id="gov-groups"></div>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/gov-client.ts';
</script>
